Block delivery to example.com / example.net / example.org.

Replaces the earlier version of this patch, which put the check at the top of
mailservice.Service.SendRendered and did `return nil`. That short-circuited
before service.Sender.SendEmail was ever called, so:

  * SendRenderedAsync saw err == nil and logged "email sent successfully",
    making the block invisible in the logs, and
  * any test that swaps in a fake Sender and waits for the delivered body
    hung forever, because the fake was never invoked.

That broke satellite/console TestWhiteLabelEmailBranding (test@example.com)
and TestNewUserNotifications (admin@example.com), both of which block on
consoleapi.ContextChannel.Get until the test context expires.

Moving the check into post.SMTPSender.SendEmail fixes both problems and is a
strictly stronger guarantee: nothing reaches a real SMTP server, including
messages sent via Service.Send, which bypasses SendRendered entirely. Fake
senders (simulate.NoMail, simulate.LinkClicker, and the EmailVerifier used in
tests) are untouched, since they are not SMTPSender.

--- a/private/post/sender.go
+++ b/private/post/sender.go
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 	"net"
 	"net/mail"
 	"net/smtp"
+	"strings"
 
 	"github.com/spacemonkeygo/monkit/v3"
 	"github.com/zeebo/errs"
@@ -37,6 +38,17 @@
 func (sender *SMTPSender) SendEmail(ctx context.Context, msg *Message) (err error) {
 	defer mon.Task()(&ctx)(&err)
 
+	// RFC 2606 reserves these domains for documentation and examples; they are
+	// never real mailboxes, so refuse to hand them to an SMTP server. Applied
+	// here rather than in mailservice so that fake senders used by tests still
+	// observe every message.
+	for _, recipient := range msg.To {
+		address := strings.ToUpper(recipient.Address)
+		if strings.HasSuffix(address, "@EXAMPLE.COM") ||
+			strings.HasSuffix(address, "@EXAMPLE.NET") ||
+			strings.HasSuffix(address, "@EXAMPLE.ORG") {
+			return nil
+		}
+	}
+
 	client, err := smtp.Dial(sender.ServerAddress)
 	if err != nil {
 		return err
